# M8 — posture rules 9 (SENTINELS) and 10 (NOT NULL) made unmatchable.
#
# HISTORY, because it changes what this mutation means. When the adversarial pass ran,
# rules 9 and 10 were NOT IMPLEMENTED at all: canon §4.8 names ten rules and the
# pre-port migrations/0005 carried eight, and nothing asserted the missing two. Both were
# implemented on 2026-10-21. So M8 is no longer "the rule is missing" — it is the standing
# proof that the rules now present cannot be hollowed out unnoticed.
#
# Rule 9 is the keystone of canon §8.1: without exactly one sentinel row per protected
# table, every tenancy denial in the suite is a read of an empty table (V18) and cannot be
# made to fail. Rule 10 is what stops a nullable user_id putting a row outside every
# policy in BOTH directions — invisible to its owner and unscoped by the predicate.
#
# Both edits are in ONE mutation because canon §4.8 lists them as one obligation, and all
# THREE named catchers must fail: sentinel missing, sentinel duplicated ("exactly one" in
# the other direction), and nullable user_id. Matching any one of them would let the other
# two rot.
# RE-POINTED FOR THE SUPABASE SHAPE. The first FIND was the bare `if n <> 1 then`, which
# was unique in the pre-port migrations/0005 (that set has since been deleted; it is in git
# history at commit b3a5f59). The port split rule 9 into 9a (owner-keyed) and 9b
# (reference tables registered in public.posture_sentinel_row), so that line now occurs
# TWICE while this mutation declares `count: 1` — and mutations/lib/mutation.mjs exits 2
# unless the target text was present EXACTLY the declared number of times. The anchor was
# therefore extended with the rule-9a message line, which is unique. It is the rule 9a leg
# that this mutation has always hollowed out; M20 is the separate control for 9b, which
# under the pre-port rule was waived and had no control at all.
id: M8
title: posture rules 9 SENTINELS and 10 NOT NULL both made unmatchable
expect: caught
proves: posture rules 9 and 10 (canon 4.8, V18) each have a control, in both directions for rule 9
expect-test: [M8] raises ST099 for the sentinel rows deleted
expect-test: [M8] raises ST099 for a SECOND row owned by the sentinel
expect-test: [M8] raises ST099 for a nullable user_id column

file: supabase/migrations/20261021140500_verify_security_posture.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
        if n <> 1 then
          bad := bad || format(E'\n  rule 9a: sentinel rows in %s: %s, expected exactly 1',
@@REPLACE
        if false then
          bad := bad || format(E'\n  rule 9a: sentinel rows in %s: %s, expected exactly 1',
@@END

file: supabase/migrations/20261021140500_verify_security_posture.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
       and not a.attnotnull
@@REPLACE
       and not a.attnotnull and false
@@END
